# Three.js guide

Rendering is draw-only. Placement, occupancy, and scenario rules live outside Three.js.

## Boundaries

- Do not decide validity, collisions, or zone rules inside scene/view code.
- Consume documents / spatial results from `showcase-package/grid-system` (or app orchestration).
- Keep helpers that only build meshes/lights/materials under `showcase-package/rendering`.

## Lifecycle & memory

- Dispose geometries, materials, textures, render targets, controls, and the renderer on teardown.
- Traverse groups and dispose child resources; do not drop references and hope GC frees GPU memory.
- Reuse materials/geometries where instances share look; prefer `InstancedMesh` / batched draws for repeats.
- Cap pixel ratio; prefer quality presets over unbounded retina cost.
- Prefer one shared clock / `setAnimationLoop` (or a single rAF owner) per showcase root.

## Assets

- Prefer compressed GLB (Draco/meshopt when needed) and budget model counts.
- Scale models to world meters; do not leave authoring-unit sizes in the scene.
- Cache loaded templates; clone for instances instead of reloading.
- Failure to load must fall back cleanly (procedural mesh is fine) without breaking the showcase.

## Frame loop

- Do not allocate heavy objects per frame (vectors, materials, geometries).
- Mutate existing objects; avoid recreating scene graphs each tick.
- Keep lights few; bake or fake shadows when marketing visuals allow.
- Skip work for hidden/disposed views; multiple `.rtls-showcase` roots must not share one global renderer state.

## Debug

- Debug grids/helpers are opt-in and must dispose with the scene.
- Prefer `renderer.info` / simple flags over permanent overlay clutter.

## Project fit

- Views live under `src/app/views/`; package rendering stays reusable and app-agnostic.
- Follow `docs/features/showcase-package/RENDERING.md` and `MODEL_LOADING.md` when touching those areas.
